import{_ as t,o as i,c as a,a3 as o}from"./chunks/framework.Dz5Q8p4s.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"From Video Generation to World-Action Models","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"From Video Generation to World-Action Models","outline":[2,2]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/tutorials/video-to-wam.md","filePath":"en/tutorials/video-to-wam.md","lastUpdated":null}'),r={name:"en/tutorials/video-to-wam.md"};function n(s,e,l,h,c,d){return i(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<h1 id="from-video-generation-to-world-action-models" tabindex="-1">From Video Generation to World-Action Models <a class="header-anchor" href="#from-video-generation-to-world-action-models" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;From Video Generation to World-Action Models&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p><strong>Full text (Chinese)</strong>: <a href="/notes/tutorials/video-to-wam">从视频生成转型 WAM</a><strong>Coverage</strong>: June 2025 – August 2026, foundational work marked <strong>†</strong>. <strong>Scale</strong>: 11 chapters + 2 appendices, 206 cited papers, all dissected individually in Chapter 11.</p></blockquote><div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">About this English page</p><p>The tutorial itself is written in Chinese. This page is its English companion: it gives the complete chapter-by-chapter structure, states what each chapter argues, and reproduces the four cross-paper conclusions that the tutorial itself calls the most valuable part. Every derivation, table, diagram and per-paper breakdown lives in the <a href="/notes/tutorials/video-to-wam">Chinese edition</a>; the <a href="/notes/en/papers/">paper library</a> is fully browsable in English.</p></div><h2 id="who-this-is-for" tabindex="-1">Who this is for <a class="header-anchor" href="#who-this-is-for" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Who this is for&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This tutorial is written for people who <strong>already work on video generation</strong> and are considering moving into world-action models. It is deliberately the mirror image of <a href="/notes/en/tutorials/world-action-models">World-Action Models</a>, which looks at WAM from the inside and assumes a robotics reader. Here every section answers one question: <em>this thing I already know how to do — does it still count in WAM, and if not, what replaces it?</em></p><h2 id="what-this-tutorial-argues" tabindex="-1">What this tutorial argues <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-this-tutorial-argues" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What this tutorial argues&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The spine is <strong>five discontinuities</strong> (Chapter 3) — the points where a habit from video generation stops working:</p><ol><li><strong>Conditioning changes from text to action.</strong> Text is a soft, global, one-shot condition; action is a hard, per-step, causally binding one.</li><li><strong>Bidirectional attention becomes doubly causal.</strong> Causal in time <em>and</em> causal with respect to the action stream.</li><li><strong>Offline batch inference becomes a closed-loop time budget.</strong> Latency stops being a nice-to-have and becomes a correctness condition.</li><li><strong>FVD and VBench give way to action following and task success.</strong> The old metrics can rank models in the wrong order.</li><li><strong>Open-loop long video becomes closed-loop error accumulation.</strong> Drift now feeds back through the policy.</li></ol><p>A second organising idea is the <strong>four jobs</strong> in a WAM stack (Chapter 4): policy, planner, evaluator/simulator, and data engine. Two of those — data engine and evaluator — need no real robot, which is why the tutorial calls them a video-generation researcher&#39;s largest immediate advantage.</p><h2 id="the-four-cross-paper-conclusions-chapter-11-0" tabindex="-1">The four cross-paper conclusions (Chapter 11.0) <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-four-cross-paper-conclusions-chapter-11-0" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The four cross-paper conclusions (Chapter 11.0)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>These are aggregated across 30 papers read end to end. They are reproduced here because they are the most transferable part of the tutorial.</p><p><strong>1. &quot;Looks good&quot; ≠ &quot;works&quot;, and the two rankings can invert.</strong> On the admission ladder, Vista scores better on generation quality (FVD 151.3, CD-FVD 51.6) than Epona (FVD 159.4, CD-FVD 86.1) — yet Epona wins on every action-robustness measure (IEC 0.54 vs 0.33, success 0.28 vs 0.08) and has a <em>trustworthy horizon</em> of 3.2 s against Vista&#39;s 1.6 s. Pick your evaluator by FVD and you pick the one you can believe for 1.6 seconds.</p><div class="tip custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Report the statistics honestly</p><p>On WorldArena, EWMScore correlates with human satisfaction at <strong>r = 0.825 over 14 models</strong> — significant. But its correlations with data-synthesis usefulness (r = 0.600) and action planning (r = 0.360) were computed over <strong>only 6 models</strong>, where two-tailed significance at p &lt; 0.05 requires |r| &gt; 0.81. Neither is significant. The defensible statement is &quot;visual quality correlates strongly with human satisfaction; its correlation with embodied utility cannot be established at this sample size&quot; — <em>not</em> &quot;looking good is unrelated to working&quot;.</p></div><p><strong>2. Most of a &quot;highly correlated&quot; evaluator&#39;s correlation usually comes from a trivial baseline.</strong> WorldGym reports per-task r = 0.78, but a predictor that <strong>only outputs the policy mean</strong> already reaches r ≈ 0.752, and within-group correlation is only r ≈ 0.465. SC3-Eval reports pooled r = 0.929, while &quot;output the criterion mean and ignore the policy entirely&quot; reaches r ≈ 0.79. RoboWorld&#39;s r = 0.989 rests on n = 8 policies. The practical instruction: <strong>the first figure in any evaluator paper should be the trivial baseline&#39;s correlation.</strong></p><p><strong>3. The marginal value of synthetic data is far below intuition, and is negative when diversity is low.</strong> DreamGen&#39;s 240k synthetic trajectories against 720 real ones (333×) yields 20.55% when trained on neural trajectories alone, versus 17.44% from just 30 real demonstrations. RoboCurate, holding the total at 10K trajectories, gets 12.5% success at 25% task diversity — <em>below</em> the 15.4% pure-real baseline — rising to 17.3% at 50% and 19.7% at 100%; action-level filtering contributes about 3.5 points against diversity&#39;s 7.2. Priority order: <strong>diversity &gt; filtering quality &gt; generation fidelity.</strong></p><p><strong>4. Pretraining matters far more than generating the future at inference time — but its source is usually misattributed.</strong> Vid2World† shows FVD 1768.8 from random init against 22.4 after transfer. Fast-WAM loses 8.0 points on RoboTwin when video co-training is removed, while inference-time future generation is worth under 1 point. The sharpest result is VPP&#39;s ablation: removing internet-scale manipulation data costs −0.36, while removing the fine-tuning of the video model on the downstream domain costs −1.02. <strong>Fine-tuning in-domain is roughly three times more important than the internet-scale prior itself.</strong></p><h2 id="chapter-outline" tabindex="-1">Chapter outline <a class="header-anchor" href="#chapter-outline" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chapter outline&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="chapter-0-—-where-you-are-standing" tabindex="-1">Chapter 0 — Where you are standing <a class="header-anchor" href="#chapter-0-—-where-you-are-standing" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chapter 0 — Where you are standing&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>0.1 The transition in one sentence</li><li>0.2 A thirty-second self-test: how far are you from WAM</li><li>0.3 Reader paths</li><li>0.4 Five sentences you should retain</li></ul><h3 id="chapter-1-—-principles-three-distributions-one-chasm" tabindex="-1">Chapter 1 — Principles: three distributions, one chasm <a class="header-anchor" href="#chapter-1-—-principles-three-distributions-one-chasm" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chapter 1 — Principles: three distributions, one chasm&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>1.1 Pinning down four terms once and for all</li><li>1.2 The WAM admission contract — the only test of whether a paper is really a WAM</li><li>1.3 Cold water first: predicting the future does not by itself confer capability</li><li>1.4 Why the move is still worth making: three pain points of VLA</li><li>1.5 A transfer map: where each of your components ends up</li></ul><h3 id="chapter-2-—-a-skills-balance-sheet" tabindex="-1">Chapter 2 — A skills balance sheet <a class="header-anchor" href="#chapter-2-—-a-skills-balance-sheet" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chapter 2 — A skills balance sheet&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>2.0 The four WAM coordinates</li><li>2.1 Assets: what transfers unchanged (about 60% of your skills)</li><li>2.2 Half-assets: what needs recalibration (about 25%)</li><li>2.3 Liabilities: intuitions you must actively discard (about 15% — and the cause of 90% of rework)</li><li>2.4 What you must learn from scratch</li></ul><h3 id="chapter-3-—-the-five-discontinuities-the-core-of-the-tutorial" tabindex="-1">Chapter 3 — The five discontinuities (the core of the tutorial) <a class="header-anchor" href="#chapter-3-—-the-five-discontinuities-the-core-of-the-tutorial" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chapter 3 — The five discontinuities (the core of the tutorial)&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>3.1 Conditioning: from text to action</li><li>3.2 Attention: bidirectional → doubly causal</li><li>3.3 Inference: offline batch → closed-loop time budget</li><li>3.4 Metrics: FVD/VBench → action following and success rate</li><li>3.5 Horizon: open-loop long video → closed-loop error accumulation</li><li>3.6 A quick-reference card for all five</li></ul><h3 id="chapter-4-—-tasks-four-jobs-two-of-which-you-can-start-today" tabindex="-1">Chapter 4 — Tasks: four jobs, two of which you can start today <a class="header-anchor" href="#chapter-4-—-tasks-four-jobs-two-of-which-you-can-start-today" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chapter 4 — Tasks: four jobs, two of which you can start today&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>4.0 The four jobs</li><li>4.1 Job 4: the data engine — your biggest dividend</li><li>4.2 Job 3: evaluator / simulator — the second-easiest entry</li><li>4.3 Job 2: planner</li><li>4.4 Job 1: policy</li><li>4.5 Three shortest paths, with prerequisites and deliverables</li></ul><h3 id="chapter-5-—-data-from-aesthetically-filtered-video-to-action-labelled-trajectories" tabindex="-1">Chapter 5 — Data: from aesthetically filtered video to action-labelled trajectories <a class="header-anchor" href="#chapter-5-—-data-from-aesthetically-filtered-video-to-action-labelled-trajectories" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chapter 5 — Data: from aesthetically filtered video to action-labelled trajectories&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>5.1 The data pyramid: WAM&#39;s biggest advantage over VLA</li><li>5.2 The commonest mistake: importing video-generation curation criteria unchanged</li><li>5.3 Five sources of action labels and the error each introduces</li><li>5.4 Dataset list, ordered by likely usefulness</li><li>5.5 A data-recipe template you can copy</li></ul><h3 id="chapter-6-—-methods-three-families-and-three-conversion-routes" tabindex="-1">Chapter 6 — Methods: three families and three conversion routes <a class="header-anchor" href="#chapter-6-—-methods-three-families-and-three-conversion-routes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chapter 6 — Methods: three families and three conversion routes&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>6.1 The three kinds of action coupling — the most important formalisation in the chapter</li><li>6.2 Route A: wrapping — the least-effort starting point</li><li>6.3 Route B: MoT dual experts — the de facto standard of 2026</li><li>6.4 Route C: deleting pixel generation — the main line of late 2026</li><li>6.5 Four &quot;pay at training, free at inference&quot; techniques</li><li>6.6 A complete comparison of action-injection interfaces</li><li>6.7 Driving WAMs at a glance</li><li>6.8 Timeline 2025.06 → 2026.08, annotated from a video-generation viewpoint</li></ul><h3 id="chapter-7-—-evaluation-rewriting-your-metric-habits" tabindex="-1">Chapter 7 — Evaluation: rewriting your metric habits <a class="header-anchor" href="#chapter-7-—-evaluation-rewriting-your-metric-habits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chapter 7 — Evaluation: rewriting your metric habits&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>7.1 The five-level pyramid</li><li>7.2 How to measure action following: the paired-intervention methodology</li><li>7.3 accuracy-at-budget: a reporting template to copy</li><li>7.4 Three crises and how to route around them</li><li>7.5 The ten evaluation mistakes video-generation people make most often</li></ul><h3 id="chapter-8-—-hands-on-eight-weeks-from-wan-cogvideox-to-a-working-wam" tabindex="-1">Chapter 8 — Hands-on: eight weeks from Wan/CogVideoX to a working WAM <a class="header-anchor" href="#chapter-8-—-hands-on-eight-weeks-from-wan-cogvideox-to-a-working-wam" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chapter 8 — Hands-on: eight weeks from Wan/CogVideoX to a working WAM&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>8.0 Step 0: fill in the four coordinates before writing code</li><li>8.1 Weeks 1–2: make the video model listen to actions</li><li>8.2 Weeks 3–4: data recipe and action labels</li><li>8.3 Weeks 5–6: attach an action head, close the loop</li><li>8.4 Weeks 7–8: produce a real conclusion</li><li>8.5 A troubleshooting table</li><li>8.6 A one-week minimal validation experiment</li></ul><h3 id="chapter-9-—-ten-research-topics-where-a-video-generation-background-is-an-advantage" tabindex="-1">Chapter 9 — Ten research topics where a video-generation background is an advantage <a class="header-anchor" href="#chapter-9-—-ten-research-topics-where-a-video-generation-background-is-an-advantage" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chapter 9 — Ten research topics where a video-generation background is an advantage&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>9.1 Porting the early-decision phenomenon from driving to manipulation</li><li>9.2 How much of the remaining horizon should be regenerated</li><li>9.3 A data-engine gate that needs no inverse dynamics model</li><li>9.4 Systematically porting the Self-Forcing family to WAM</li><li>9.5 A calibrated measure of action following</li><li>9.6 What else does a video VAE encode for free</li><li>9.7 Making &quot;sparse futures&quot; learnable</li><li>9.8 A general generated-video-to-action translator with a rejection mechanism</li><li>9.9 Benchmarking long-video memory mechanisms on WAM memory tasks</li><li>9.10 Filling in the λ problem</li><li>9.11 Three further gaps exposed by the Chapter 11 close reading</li><li>9.12 Three disciplines for choosing a topic</li></ul><h3 id="chapter-10-—-paper-list" tabindex="-1">Chapter 10 — Paper list <a class="header-anchor" href="#chapter-10-—-paper-list" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chapter 10 — Paper list&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Thirteen thematic groups, from the ten must-reads for the transition, through causalisation and acceleration on the video-generation side, conversion routes, action injection, the three families, efficiency, data engines, evaluation, driving, memory and agentic outer loops, failure and counterfactuals, RL inside world models, to theory and surveys.</p><h3 id="chapter-11-—-deep-dive-paper-by-paper" tabindex="-1">Chapter 11 — Deep dive, paper by paper <a class="header-anchor" href="#chapter-11-—-deep-dive-paper-by-paper" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chapter 11 — Deep dive, paper by paper&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>11.0 Four cross-paper conclusions (summarised above)</li><li>11.1 Six template conversions of a video model into a WAM</li><li>11.2 Nine representative works from the three families</li><li>11.3 Three representative works on the action interface</li><li>11.4 Six representative data engines</li><li>11.5 Six representative evaluation papers</li><li>11.6 The previous five sections compressed into a copy-this checklist</li><li>11.7 Every remaining paper dissected in chronological order</li></ul><h3 id="appendices" tabindex="-1">Appendices <a class="header-anchor" href="#appendices" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Appendices&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>Appendix A — Concept cross-reference: terminology, WAM-specific vocabulary (English–Chinese), and a one-page decision table</li><li>Appendix B — Transition FAQ: twenty common misconceptions</li></ul><h2 id="related-tutorials" tabindex="-1">Related tutorials <a class="header-anchor" href="#related-tutorials" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Related tutorials&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="/notes/en/tutorials/world-action-models">World-Action Models</a> — the same subject seen from inside robotics</li><li><a href="/notes/en/tutorials/vla">Vision-Language-Action Models</a> — the policy family WAM is measured against</li><li><a href="/notes/en/tutorials/autoregressive">Autoregressive Video Generation</a> — the causal machinery you are being asked to reuse</li></ul>',45)])])}const m=t(r,[["render",n]]);export{p as __pageData,m as default};
